@sama Sure but sometimes you need a different path. 

Alchemy wasn’t correct. You needed chemistry.

In any case, hope is not a business model.

@tracewoodgrains their valuation hasn’t diminished but their aura certainly has, and they have delayed the IPO, likely because the numbers don’t really justify the valuation. Oracle’s valuation has a taken hit from the dependency on OAI, OAI have hinted at bailouts (e.g data center backstops) and I still think OAI is in jeopardy.

timing on these things is very hard to predict, of course. @edzitron may have messed up the timing but the spirit of what he and I have said remains true: they are burning a ton of cash and (still) don’t really have a compelling story around how they might ever become systematically profitable.

simply dragging on Ed’s timing without seeing the larger picture is thus not really fair, IMHO.

@Davidedeferrar @gabriberton At a general level, yes.
But LLMs are auto-regressive at the level of a single discrete token, which typically contains less than 20 bits of information.
The state of your brain is high-dimensional, continuous, and way more informative than 20 bits.

@tracewoodgrains 1. i never called a “dip” in AI development, even once.  

2. I said that we would get stuck if we didn’t use symbols. we did, then people started using symbol-manipulation and things got going again.

3. You are making stuff, so we are done.

@HeinrichKuttler @sapinker i am asking for specifics; from what i can tell she has defended herself ably against a bunch of ad hominem. not remotely saying she is right, but the replies to her have mostly been very weak and contentless.

you also ignored the rest of what I said about why your post was unfair and unwarranted. 

my full critique of misinterpretations around Astra is here: https://t.co/srDjSHx5kM with a supplement a day later that makes my argument significantly stronger: https://t.co/c1BMOVt7Po

@penadev @gabriberton No. I'm arguing against LLMs as the central engine of human-level intelligent systems.
I, along with many others (like Demis Hassabis), believe that the centerpiece of future AI systems will be world models.
LLMs will be a component, mostly for textual input and output.

